  "title": "Chapter 14 – Engineering Materials and Their Properties",
  "order": 13,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "1vvc4ge",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Engineering materials are broadly classified into Metals, Ceramics, Polymers, and Composites.",
        "Ferrous metals contain iron as their major constituent, whereas non-ferrous metals lack iron.",
        "Mechanical properties such as tensile strength, ductility, hardness, and toughness govern structural performance under applied loads.",
        "Physical properties like thermal conductivity, electrical resistivity, density, and melting point dictate operational limits.",
        "Alloying elements are added to pure metals to significantly enhance strength, corrosion resistance, and heat behavior."
      ],
      "id": "dyoiwgo"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Material Classes",
          "value": "4 Groups",
          "icon": "book"
